# Upstream sprite sources, not game assets. These are third-party checkouts
# kept alongside the project for reference:
#   opentibia_sprite_pack/ 48 MB, contains its own .git
#   tibia_sprites_repo/     7 MB, contains its own .git
#   tibia_sprites_zip/     13 MB, 9225 files
# The first two carry a nested .git, so `git add` records them as gitlinks --
# a reference to a commit that does not exist in this repository. That is a
# broken submodule, not a backup, and it cannot be cloned usefully.
# The processed sprites the client actually loads live in client/assets/.
opentibia_sprite_pack/
tibia_sprites_repo/
tibia_sprites_zip/

# Screenshots written by tools/boss_sprite_shots.js. Regenerable, and they are
# 300-700 KB each, so they do not belong in history.
tmp_shots/

# Third-party import that must not be published. See docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md.
#
#   client/assets/rpg-import/   67 files, 56.3 MB, from wekax2004/RPG
#
# The source repository has no LICENSE file (GitHub reports "license": null), so
# these files must not be distributed. docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md has said that since
# the import was staged; this entry is the mechanism that was missing, which is
# why the directory was committed in the initial commit and has been on
# origin/main since 2026-09-25.
#
# This entry does NOT untrack files that are already tracked -- git ignores
# .gitignore for anything already in the index. Removing them from the repository
# tip needs `git rm --cached -r client/assets/rpg-import` and a commit; removing
# them from published history needs a filter-repo rewrite and a force-push. Both
# are the owner's call. See PROJECT.md, Known Licence Issues.
#
# Listed here anyway so that a file added to this directory from now on is not
# silently staged by `git add`.
client/assets/rpg-import/

